<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=photography">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Photography</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1826 or 1827</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Photography, whose name means 'drawing of light', is the capture of camera images on light-sensitive materials; the first such image to be fixed was made by the French scientist Nicéphore Niépce in 1826 or 1827, after an exposure of hours or days. His partner Louis Daguerre developed the daguerreotype, the first publicly announced and commercially viable process, whose details were made public in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in August 1839. Henry Fox Talbot's developed-out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> halide negative process, which allowed many prints from one negative, remains the basic technology of chemical film cameras.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/photography.jpg" alt="Photography" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Nicéphore Niépce · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_the_Window_at_Le_Gras,_Joseph_Nic%C3%A9phore_Ni%C3%A9pce.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1826–1827</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Le Gras, France</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Nicéphore Niépce · Louis Daguerre · <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>England (Henry Fox Talbot) (1835); Brazil (Hercule Florence) (1832–1833)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The article notes that Johann Heinrich Schulze's fleeting images of stencilled letters, about 1717, are 'sometimes regarded as the very first form of photography', that Thomas Wedgwood made the first reliably documented attempt to capture camera images around 1800 but could not fix them, and that Hippolyte Bayard developed a method of his own but was not recognised as its inventor because he delayed announcing it.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/camera-obscura">Camera obscura<span>4th century BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/cinematograph">Cinematograph<span>1892</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=photography">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
